<section class="band tight">
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow">
    <ol class="steps">

      <li>
        <h3>Clone the repo and prove it runs</h3>
        <p>
          This is the current <code>ezgraph-demo</code> tree — NestJS + Fastify, three graphs,
          and both sides of the benchmark. Ignore the HTTP host for now. The graph lives under
          <code>src/graphs/quote-graph/</code>.
        </p>

        <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:12px">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>git clone https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo.git</code>
        </div>
        <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:12px">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>cd ezgraph-demo &amp;&amp; npm install</code>
        </div>
        <div class="install" style="margin-bottom:12px">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>cp .env.example .env  # then set one provider API key</code>
        </div>
        <div class="install">
          <span class="prompt">$</span>
          <code>npm run test:quote-graph</code>
        </div>

        <p style="margin-top:20px">
          That script runs the scripted unit tests and the live e2e file. Without a provider
          key the e2e case <b>skips</b>; the unit suite still drives a full quote — driver,
          vehicle, history, coverage, adjust, accept — against a fake gateway. No network.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <span class="label">What you just proved</span>
          <p>
            The graph is not a slide. It compiles, it routes, it persists node-local state,
            and it rejects a financed car with liability-only coverage. That is the product
            the <a href="/ezgraph/compare/langgraph/">benchmark</a> measures.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li id="live">
