      <li>
        <h3>Orient — five stages, one folder</h3>
        <p>
          Open <a href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/tree/main/src/graphs/quote-graph">src/graphs/quote-graph/</a>.
          The layout is the first tutorial's four files, grown up:
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/tree/main/src/graphs/quote-graph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">src/graphs/quote-graph/</a>
          </div>
<pre><code>quote-graph.ts              graph definition + idle expiry
quote-graph.state.ts        domain types + per-node slices
nodes/driver.node.ts        stage 1 — identity and license
nodes/vehicle.node.ts       stage 2 — catalog lookup + use
nodes/history.node.ts       stage 3 — incidents, own history space
nodes/coverage.node.ts      stage 4 — selections + rating
nodes/quote.node.ts         stage 5 — present, adjust, accept
backend/                  catalog, rating engine, clock
prompt/quote-prompt.ts      copy the model sees</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          Driver waits until it has a valid driver, then <code>advance(VehicleNode)</code>.
          Vehicle, history, and coverage do the same. Quote can stay, go backward, or
          <code>finish()</code>. Same four outcomes as weather.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>The contract did not change</h3>
        <p>
          Compare this to <code>CollectNode.nextStep</code> from
          <a href="/ezgraph/tutorial/">tutorial 1</a>. Quit first, then domain success, else stay.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/nodes/driver.node.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nodes/driver.node.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 118–130</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>protected nextStep(
  _state: QuoteGraphStateType,
  context: DriverContext,
  conversation: ConversationNodeRunResult,
): GraphNodeUpdate&lt;QuoteGraphStateType&gt; {
  if (conversation.quitRequested) return this.quit(conversation);
  if (context.driver) {
    return this.advance(VehicleNode, conversation).withState({
      driver: context.driver,
    });
  }
  return this.stay(conversation);
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          What grew is the <em>handler</em>, not the contract. <code>capture_driver</code>
          rejects a bad state code, a suspended license, an age under 16, and licensed-years
          that cannot be true — then writes <code>context.driver</code>. Weather had nothing
          to validate; insurance does. The framework still never sees that domain.
        </p>

        <div class="callout warn">
          <span class="label">Two style differences vs tutorial 1</span>
          <p>
            This graph still uses <code>DriverNode.name</code> when it builds state, and the
            handlers mutate <code>context</code> then return <code>{ output, stopAfterBatch }</code>.
            The first tutorial teaches <code>.id()</code> and
            <code>toolResult().withContext().haltAfterBatch()</code>. Both work. Prefer the
            tutorial-1 style in new graphs; do not "fix" this one while you are learning it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>State is still one annotation</h3>
        <p>
          Each stage owns a slice. Two nodes cannot write the same key — a collision is a
          type error, which is the point of the homepage state comparison.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/quote-graph.state.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">quote-graph.state.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 63–81</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>export type QuoteGraphNodes = {
  DriverNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{ driver?: DriverProfile }&gt;;
  VehicleNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{
    resolvedVehicleId?: string;
    vehicle?: VehicleUse;
  }&gt;;
  HistoryNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{ history?: InsuranceHistory }&gt;;
  CoverageNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{ coverage?: CoverageSelection }&gt;;
  QuoteNode?: NodeStateValue&lt;{
    tiers?: QuoteTier[];
    acceptedTier?: QuoteTierName;
    referenceNumber?: string;
  }&gt;;
};

export const QuoteGraphState = createGraphStateAnnotation(
  DriverNode.name,
  () =&gt; ({} as QuoteGraphNodes),
);</code></pre>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>History spaces — three channels, not one transcript</h3>
        <p>
          Collection chatter must not sit in the quote-presentation window. Incidents get
          their own channel. The definition declares that; nodes seed the next channel when
          they advance.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/quote-graph.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">quote-graph.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 36–44</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>initialHistorySpace: "quote-intake",
historySpaces: [
  [DriverNode, "quote-intake"],
  [VehicleNode, "quote-intake"],
  [HistoryNode, "quote-incidents"],
  [CoverageNode, "quote-intake"],
  [QuoteNode, "quote-present"],
  [TerminateSessionNode, "quote-terminal"],
],</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/nodes/vehicle.node.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nodes/vehicle.node.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 151–160</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>return this.advance(HistoryNode, conversation)
  .withState({
    resolvedVehicleId: context.use.vehicleId,
    vehicle: context.use,
  })
  .withHistory(
    "quote-incidents",
    new HumanMessage("Collect the driving and insurance history."),
  );</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          Coverage does the same into <code>quote-present</code> and also
          <code>withStateFor(QuoteNode, { tiers })</code> — the next stage's local state is
          written by the stage that computed it.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>Domain stays out of the graph</h3>
        <p>
          <code>VehicleCatalog</code> and <code>RatingEngine</code> are ordinary TypeScript
          modules. Nodes call them from handlers. The LangGraph twin imports the same
          backends — that is why the <a href="/ezgraph/compare/langgraph/">benchmark</a> can subtract them
          and measure only the framework layer.
        </p>

        <ul class="checks">
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b><code>resolve_vehicle</code></b> asks the catalog; multiple trims come
              back as <code>needsTrim</code>, not as a guess.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b><code>select_coverage</code></b> runs
              <code>validateCoverageSelection</code> — a financed car cannot be liability-only
              — then <code>RatingEngine.quoteTiers</code>.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.5 4.5L6 12 2.5 8.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
            <span><b><code>quote-clock.ts</code></b> is how tests freeze "today" at
              <code>2027-06-01</code> so incident windows and premiums stay deterministic.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>Going backward — and paying for a stronger model once</h3>
        <p>
          "Actually, raise my deductible" is the transition hand-rolled graphs get wrong.
          QuoteNode either stays with a recomputed tier list, finishes, or sends the customer
          <em>and their current message</em> back to coverage.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/nodes/quote.node.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nodes/quote.node.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 93–98 · 223–227</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>getLlmConfig(): GraphLlmConfigOverride {
  return ModelCatalog.model("openai:gpt-5.1", {
    retries: 3,
    reasoningEffort: "low",
  });
}

if (context.revise) {
  return this.advance(CoverageNode, conversation).forwardInput(
    state,
    "Review and update the coverage selections.",
  );
}</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          Intake runs on <code>gpt-4o-mini</code> (the graph default). Only presentation
          overrides. <code>forwardInput()</code> marks the customer's message unconsumed and
          copies it into coverage's history space — the field the LangGraph twin has to set
          by hand on every return path.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>Idle expiry is a restore hook, not a framework flag</h3>
        <p>
          Rates are not held forever. After 30 minutes of silence the next turn starts over.
          There is no <code>expireAfter</code> in EZGraph — you implement
          <code>onRestoreSessionDoc</code> and use <code>idleMs()</code>. That is pitfall #7
          from the first tutorial, in the file.
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/src/graphs/quote-graph/quote-graph.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">quote-graph.ts</a>
            <span class="note">lines 52–75</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>protected override async onRestoreSessionDoc(
  sessionDoc: SessionDocument&lt;QuoteGraphStateType&gt;,
): Promise&lt;SessionDocument&lt;QuoteGraphStateType&gt; | null&gt; {
  if (this.idleMs(sessionDoc) &gt;= readMs("QUOTE_GRAPH_IDLE_MS", DEFAULT_IDLE_MS)) {
    return null;
  }
  return sessionDoc;
}

protected buildGraph() {
  const graph = this.createStateGraph(QuoteGraphState);
  graph.registerTurnNodes(
    DriverNode,
    VehicleNode,
    HistoryNode,
    CoverageNode,
    QuoteNode,
    TerminateSessionNode,
  );
  graph.configAutoRoute();
  graph.addEdge(TerminateSessionNode, END);
  return graph.compile();
}</code></pre>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h3>Read the test you already ran</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/test/quote-graph/quote-graph.spec.ts">test/quote-graph/quote-graph.spec.ts</a>
          scripts the model and asserts on <code>currentNode</code>, node-local state, and
          the rating engine's numbers. The first turns look like this:
        </p>

        <div class="code">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
            <a class="file" href="https://github.com/picoflowio/ezgraph-demo/blob/main/test/quote-graph/quote-graph.spec.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">test/quote-graph/quote-graph.spec.ts</a>
            <span class="note good">no key, no network</span>
          </div>
<pre><code>state = await invoke(graph, undefined,
  "I need a quote for my car. I'm Jamie Rivera…");
assert.equal(state.currentNode, "VehicleNode");
assert.equal(state.nodes.DriverNode?.driver?.fullName, "Jamie Rivera");

state = await invoke(graph, state, "I drive a 2019 Toyota Camry.");
assert.equal(state.currentNode, "VehicleNode");   // still here — needs a trim
assert.match(state.response, /LE, SE, and XSE/);

state = await invoke(graph, state,
  "It's the SE. I finance it…");
assert.equal(state.currentNode, "HistoryNode");</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
          Later turns reject liability-only on a financed car, accept $500 deductibles, adjust
          to $1000, and finish with a <code>QT-######</code> reference. A second test stays
          in <code>DriverNode</code> when the license is suspended. That is the "schema
          rejection becomes a follow-up, not a 500" lesson from tutorial 1, with a real rule.
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <span class="label">These tests call <code>graph.invoke</code> directly</span>
          <p>
            They predate <code>createTurnHarness</code> from
            <a href="/ezgraph/docs/developer-guide/">§15</a>. They still prove routing and
            domain rules; they do not exercise lease / restore / persist the way the weather
            harness does. When you add a stage, prefer the harness for new tests.
          </p>
